{"$message_type":"diagnostic","message":"type annotations needed","code":{"code":"E0283","explanation":"The compiler could not infer a type and asked for a type annotation.\n\nErroneous code example:\n\n```compile_fail,E0283\nlet x = \"hello\".chars().rev().collect();\n```\n\nThis error indicates that type inference did not result in one unique possible\ntype, and extra information is required. In most cases this can be provided\nby adding a type annotation. Sometimes you need to specify a generic type\nparameter manually.\n\nA common example is the `collect` method on `Iterator`. It has a generic type\nparameter with a `FromIterator` bound, which for a `char` iterator is\nimplemented by `Vec` and `String` among others. Consider the following snippet\nthat reverses the characters of a string:\n\nIn the first code example, the compiler cannot infer what the type of `x` should\nbe: `Vec<char>` and `String` are both suitable candidates. To specify which type\nto use, you can use a type annotation on `x`:\n\n```\nlet x: Vec<char> = \"hello\".chars().rev().collect();\n```\n\nIt is not necessary to annotate the full type. Once the ambiguity is resolved,\nthe compiler can infer the rest:\n\n```\nlet x: Vec<_> = \"hello\".chars().rev().collect();\n```\n\nAnother way to provide the compiler with enough information, is to specify the\ngeneric type parameter:\n\n```\nlet x = \"hello\".chars().rev().collect::<Vec<char>>();\n```\n\nAgain, you need not specify the full type if the compiler can infer it:\n\n```\nlet x = \"hello\".chars().rev().collect::<Vec<_>>();\n```\n\nWe can see a self-contained example below:\n\n```compile_fail,E0283\nstruct Foo;\n\nimpl Into<u32> for Foo {\n    fn into(self) -> u32 { 1 }\n}\n\nlet foo = Foo;\nlet bar: u32 = foo.into() * 1u32;\n```\n\nThis error can be solved by adding type annotations that provide the missing\ninformation to the compiler. In this case, the solution is to specify the\ntrait's type parameter:\n\n```\nstruct Foo;\n\nimpl Into<u32> for Foo {\n    fn into(self) -> u32 { 1 }\n}\n\nlet foo = Foo;\nlet bar: u32 = Into::<u32>::into(foo) * 1u32;\n```\n"},"level":"error","spans":[{"file_name":"src\\util.rs","byte_start":2386,"byte_end":2405,"line_start":83,"line_end":83,"column_start":14,"column_end":33,"is_primary":true,"text":[{"text":"            .GetCurrentPatternAs(windows::Win32::UI::Accessibility::UIA_InvokePatternId)","highlight_start":14,"highlight_end":33}],"label":"cannot infer type of the type parameter `T` declared on the method `GetCurrentPatternAs`","suggested_replacement":null,"suggestion_applicability":null,"expansion":null}],"children":[{"message":"cannot satisfy `_: Interface`","code":null,"level":"note","spans":[],"children":[],"rendered":null},{"message":"the following types implement trait `Interface`:\n  AsyncIAdviseSink\n  AsyncIAdviseSink2\n  AsyncIMultiQI\n  AsyncIPipeByte\n  AsyncIPipeDouble\n  AsyncIPipeLong\n  AsyncIUnknown\n  IAccIdentity\nand 250 others","code":null,"level":"help","spans":[],"children":[],"rendered":null},{"message":"required by a bound in `IUIAutomationElement::GetCurrentPatternAs`","code":null,"level":"note","spans":[{"file_name":"C:\\Users\\trant\\.cargo\\registry\\src\\index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f\\windows-0.58.0\\src\\Windows\\Win32\\UI\\Accessibility\\mod.rs","byte_start":273068,"byte_end":273087,"line_start":4161,"line_end":4161,"column_start":19,"column_end":38,"is_primary":false,"text":[{"text":"    pub unsafe fn GetCurrentPatternAs<T>(&self, patternid: UIA_PATTERN_ID) -> windows_core::Result<T>","highlight_start":19,"highlight_end":38}],"label":"required by a bound in this associated function","suggested_replacement":null,"suggestion_applicability":null,"expansion":null},{"file_name":"C:\\Users\\trant\\.cargo\\registry\\src\\index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f\\windows-0.58.0\\src\\Windows\\Win32\\UI\\Accessibility\\mod.rs","byte_start":273173,"byte_end":273196,"line_start":4163,"line_end":4163,"column_start":12,"column_end":35,"is_primar